(4. The Trias Politica) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Montesquieu published his _De l?Esprit des Lois_ in 1748. An English) Tj T*
(translation can be found on the internet, and a short biographical) Tj T*
(note, taken from there, has been included in an appendix. Though his) Tj T*
(book discusses many issues, it remained famous for the theory of the) Tj T*
(separation of powers, i.e. of the Legislative, Executive and Judicial) Tj T*
(branches of government. The American phrase is ?checks and balances?. A) Tj T*
(key passage in Book XI shows that Montesquieu also refers to the) Tj T*
(existing case of England - so that his role is not one of originator) Tj T*
(but one of keen observer and developer of theory:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?One nation there is also in the world that has for the direct end of) Tj T*
(its constitution political liberty. We shall presently examine the) Tj T*
(principles on which this liberty is founded; if they are sound, liberty) Tj T*
(will appear in its highest perfection.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(To discover political liberty in a constitution, no great labour is) Tj T*
(requisite. If we are capable of seeing it where it exists, it is soon) Tj T*
(found, and we need not go far in search of it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(6. Of the Constitution of England. In every government there are three) Tj T*
(sorts of power: the legislative; the executive in respect to things) Tj T*
(dependent on the law of nations; and the executive in regard to matters) Tj T*
(that depend on the civil law.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
