(By virtue of the first, the prince or magistrate enacts temporary or) Tj T*
(perpetual laws, and amends or abrogates those that have been already) Tj T*
(enacted. By the second, he makes peace or war, sends or receives) Tj T*
(embassies, establishes the public security, and provides against) Tj T*
(invasions. By the third, he punishes criminals, or determines the) Tj T*
(disputes that arise between individuals. The latter we shall call the) Tj T*
(judiciary power, and the other simply the executive power of the state.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The political liberty of the subject is a tranquillity of mind arising) Tj T*
(from the opinion each person has of his safety. In order to have this) Tj T*
(liberty, it is requisite the government be so constituted as one man) Tj T*
(need not be afraid of another.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(When the legislative and executive powers are united in the same) Tj T*
(person, or in the same body of magistrates, there can be no liberty;) Tj T*
(because apprehensions may arise, lest the same monarch or senate should) Tj T*
(enact tyrannical laws, to execute them in a tyrannical manner.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Again, there is no liberty, if the judiciary power be not separated) Tj T*
(from the legislative and executive. Were it joined with the) Tj T*
(legislative, the life and liberty of the subject would be exposed to) Tj T*
(arbitrary control; for the judge would be then the legislator.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Were it joined to the executive power, the judge might behave with) Tj T*
(violence and oppression.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There would be an end of everything, were the same man or the same) Tj T*
(body, whether of the nobles or of the people, to exercise those three) Tj T*
(powers, that of enacting laws, that of executing the public) Tj T*
(resolutions, and of trying the causes of individuals.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is useful to recall Montesquieu?s definition of political liberty:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?We must have continually present to our minds the difference between) Tj T*
(independence and liberty. Liberty is a right of doing whatever the laws) Tj T*
(permit, and if a citizen could do what they forbid he would be no) Tj T*
(longer possessed of liberty, because all his fellow-citizens would have) Tj T*
(the same power.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Thus, of key importance: A person with few means can take less) Tj T*
(advantage of his liberties than a person with more means. A person with) Tj T*
(insufficient means might be regarded as not free at all. This brings us) Tj T*
(to the economic amendment to Montesquieu?s heritage.) Tj T*
