() Tj T*
(In the years after World War II, Western societies created systems of) Tj T*
(social security - the ?Welfare State? - and for a while it seemed as if) Tj T*
(they could do so without serious economic consequences. From a) Tj T*
(macro-economic point of view, they hoped to enjoy growth, full) Tj T*
(employment and low inflation. These indeed happened in the golden years) Tj T*
(1950-1970. However, there arose the problem of stagflation around 1970,) Tj T*
(i.e. the combination of high unemployment, high inflation and) Tj T*
(stagnating growth. Around 1980, unemployment and inflation reached) Tj T*
(double digit values. Other economic indicators in the red were budget) Tj T*
(deficits, high interest rates, and the crowding out of private) Tj T*
(investments. Adjustment to these problems has been difficult and slow.) Tj T*
(The economic performance around 2004 is a major improvement from the) Tj T*
(worst episode, but the progress seems to be stagnating. The ongoing) Tj T*
(discussion in policy making circles during all these years is how the) Tj T*
(Welfare State arrangements are related to these economic problems, and) Tj T*
(what the proper policy reaction should be.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Welfare state economics differs from ?traditional? macro-economics in) Tj T*
(that there are more arrangements that protect individuals from) Tj T*
(insecurity and that entitle them to benefits. Welfare state economics) Tj T*
(however does not differ from ?traditional? macro-economics in the) Tj T*
(respect that the basic laws of economics cannot be changed. Generous as) Tj T*
(arrangements can be, people fundamentally still react to incentives.) Tj T*
(Welfare state arrangements tend to reduce the base of the economy of) Tj T*
(those participating in the workforce and they increase the burden on) Tj T*
(those. The welfare state also tends to generate more unemployment and) Tj T*
(inflation. While unemployment would ?traditionally? cause people to) Tj T*
