(Structure of the argument) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The crucial insight is that the people who can demand pay rises need) Tj T*
(not be the people who run the risk of unemployment thereof. High) Tj T*
(productivity workers run less risk of unemployment and can more easily) Tj T*
(demand pay rises, while low productivity workers run the larger risk of) Tj T*
(unemployment. High productivity workers are more versatile and are able) Tj T*
(to shift the risk of unemployment to the lower income groups. When jobs) Tj T*
(are scarce, the high productivity workers even crowd out others from) Tj T*
(the labour market. [40]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The policy rule on taxes is: don?t tax low productivity labour. Why ?) Tj T*
(To keep it employed so that more productive labour will meet more) Tj T*
(competition and will not demand inflationary pay rises. In Europe,) Tj T*
(taxes on low productive labour are still high, causing a high minimum) Tj T*
(wage that causes unemployment. These taxes could be abolished, and) Tj T*
(without costs, since these workers are unemployed anyway. Similarly,) Tj T*
(marginal tax rates are less a problem than often said. The proposed) Tj T*
(alternative policy provides an improvement on both unemployment and) Tj T*
(inflation, exactly the kind of policy measure required for in the) Tj T*
(current situation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This analysis is not common knowledge. It is missing in the economic) Tj T*
(journals, it is missing for example in Borjas?s \(1996\) much used) Tj T*
(textbook for undergraduates. Borjas \(1996:441\) states: ?The minimum) Tj T*
(wage, however, affects mainly less-skilled young workers, so it is) Tj T*
(difficult to attribute much of the unemployment problem to minimum wage) Tj T*
(legislation.?  [41] For policy makers, the OECD \(1998\) reports: ?The) Tj T*
(cross-country evidence suggests that the minimum wage has no) Tj T*
(significant impact on overall adult employment.?  though OECD \(2000\) is) Tj T*
(more guarded, see chapter 44. We will show however that a minimum wage) Tj T*
(can have huge ?multipliers?.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The difference that it means) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is useful to clarify the difference between currect macro-economic) Tj T*
(policy in Western nations and what macro-economic policy can be) Tj T*
(according to this book.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Current macro-economic policy:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(· accepts unemployment as a consequence of low inflation and reduced) Tj T*
(deficits) Tj T*
