(How has the tax void come about ? Since abolishing the tax void does) Tj T*
(not cost anything, and would generate a lot of employment, why don?t we) Tj T*
(abolish it ? Why do we continue the present absurd situation of mass) Tj T*
(unemployment ?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It appears that the situation has come about gradually, by a mechanism) Tj T*
(that is difficult to observe directly. It involves the co-ordination of) Tj T*
(tax policy with social policy, specifically the indexation of taxes and) Tj T*
(subsistence.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(First note that OECD countries adjust their taxes for inflation, see) Tj T*
(OECD \(1986\). Tax exemption in 2002 will often be close to the) Tj T*
(inflation-adjusted real value of 1950. On the other hand, research in) Tj T*
(social psychology shows that subsistence tends to rise with the general) Tj T*
(level of income, the growth of which consists of inflation and real) Tj T*
(growth \(or real net income\). So there is ?differential indexation?. In) Tj T*
(the 1950s exemption was pretty close to subsistence, so that there was) Tj T*
(no void to speak of. Since then, exemption has lagged behind the) Tj T*
(standard of living. When tax exemption lags behind net subsistence,) Tj T*
(then there is a multiplier effect on gross subsistence, with an) Tj T*
(accelerated increase of the tax void. This process also explains the) Tj T*
(?squeezing of income differentials? in OECD countries.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Holland is the example again. In 1951, exemption for a single person) Tj T*
(household was ? 354 and for a couple without childern ? 463. At that) Tj T*
(time there was no official minimum wage, but it can be taken at that) Tj T*
(value. The price level in 2002 \(1951=1\) is 6.25 and the wage index 2002) Tj T*
