(Definitions can be devious in quite vulgar ways. In the English) Tj T*
(economics literature, ?perfect competition? is defined as the situation) Tj T*
(when no agent can affect the price, i.e. all agents are price takers.) Tj T*
(The Dutch word for this case is ?full competition?. The English) Tj T*
(definition forces English economists to use the word ?imperfection? for) Tj T*
(all other cases. Even quite reasonable cases, in the normal state of) Tj T*
(human life, when agents have market power but balance at some social) Tj T*
(optimum, would be ?imperfect?. Also a natural monopoly would be an) Tj T*
(imperfection - even if one could not conceive the situation differently) Tj T*
(since the monopoly is a natural one. It would be better if the English) Tj T*
(economists would adopt the Dutch definition, so that the words) Tj T*
(?perfect? and ?imperfect? could be used in their proper sense depending) Tj T*
(upon circumstance. This is just a vulgar example of how definitions can) Tj T*
(lead one astray.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The competition of alternative concepts can be quite sophisticated) Tj T*
(however. Let us illustrate this with three examples. The most) Tj T*
(illuminating example may well be Pythagoras?s theorem and its relation) Tj T*
(to the circle. This problem concerns mathematics, so that the) Tj T*
(discussion is less taxed by semantics and empirical matters - though) Tj T*
(there is of course the theory about empirical space. The second example) Tj T*
(of ?falsification? is surely in the realm of empirics. The third) Tj T*
(example concerns the distinction between determinism and volition.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Pythagoras and the circle) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Regard a triangle with perpendicular sides _a _and _b _and hypotenuse) Tj T*
(_c. _There are two points of view:) Tj T*
