(Let us regard policy simulations using common practical economic) Tj T*
(models. Let us for example regard the effects of a rise of government) Tj T*
(investments as financed by taxes, for a sustained period of 8 years) Tj T*
(\(two presidential terms\). To do a simulation properly, the tax function) Tj T*
(used must reflect government policy, which includes indexation. For) Tj T*
(example, exemption and other brackets are adjusted for last year) Tj T*
(inflation while the statutory marginal rates remain the same. The) Tj T*
(different investment paths result in different paths for the taxes.) Tj T*
(This is not just a model result, but also the agents in the economy) Tj T*
(would encouter different regimes. Thus the model generates different) Tj T*
(_dynamic_ marginal rates, while the agents are assumed to react only to) Tj T*
(the same \(_static_\) rates. The situation gets even complexer when the) Tj T*
(alternative policy includes a different indexation scheme, such as) Tj T*
(indexation of taxes on national income. All this means, then, that we) Tj T*
(are justified in doubting the validity of current modeling practices.) Tj T*
(Modelers should start wondering about this kind of dynamic consistency) Tj T*
(\(not to be confused with the ?dynamic consistency of policy? as another) Tj T*
(topic in economic literature on ?credibility?\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
