(Off balanced growth) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Income growth means a shift of the employment density or the earnings) Tj T*
(distribution. Earlier we looked at income distributions for Holland) Tj T*
(1950 and 1988, and the reader may now better understand why. The Dutch) Tj T*
(distributions could be approximated by lognormal distributions, but the) Tj T*
(mean, variance and the size of the labour force changed. Taxes also) Tj T*
(have been indexed on inflation instead of income. So we may surmise) Tj T*
(that there was no balanced growth.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(How do agents react when there is no balanced growth ? Indexation to) Tj T*
(national income can be said to be ?neutral to the income change?. The) Tj T*
(tax choices facing an individual, whose income grows as national) Tj T*
(income, are constant. The utility reaction thus depends on the change) Tj T*
(of income itself. It may be that an individual, whose income might grow) Tj T*
(as fast as national income, decides to grow differently, either more or) Tj T*
(less, depending upon his leisure-income utility. Since the context is) Tj T*
(that all individuals are adjusting, this may be reformulated as that) Tj T*
(individuals are determining their place within the income distribution.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Our analysis thus suggests that tax incentives primarily affect) Tj T*
(decisions about one?s place in the income density. Any individual) Tj T*
(change that differs from the national average can be interpreted, or) Tj T*
(defined, as the individual decision to accept another place in the) Tj T*
(income distribution. It would be interesting to reinterprete economic) Tj T*
(models on growth in these terms, and see whether elegant regularities) Tj T*
(can be found or constructed. However, it leads too far to really look) Tj T*
(into this matter, since it is not our proper subject.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
