(31. Differential impact of the minimum wage on exposed and sheltered) Tj T*
(sectors) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Some sectors of the economy are exposed to foreign competition and some) Tj T*
(are sheltered from it. These exposed and sheltered sectors are likely) Tj T*
(to have a different composition of their labour force, notably) Tj T*
(different rates of dependency on the minimum wage. If a national) Tj T*
(incomes policy does not respect these differences, a country can have) Tj T*
(both unemployment and a surplus on the trade account.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Introduction) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The two Oil Crises in the 1970s created a problem for the Dutch economy) Tj T*
(which has become known in the literature as the so-called ?Dutch) Tj T*
(Disease?. When the price of a nationally produced but internationally) Tj T*
(traded resource rises - and this happened since Holland is rich in) Tj T*
(natural gas and a free rider of OPEC - then this causes the exchange) Tj T*
(rate to rise, and then this indirectly causes a reduction of the other) Tj T*
(exports and an increase in competing imports. Thus the original) Tj T*
(increase in national wealth paradoxically combines with an increase in) Tj T*
(unemployment - and eventually a lower growth path.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This chapter concerns the Dutch policy reaction to that Dutch Disease.) Tj T*
(If policy is not targetted at stabilisation of the exchange rate by) Tj T*
(monetary means and capital flows, but at tinkering with the labour) Tj T*
(market, then the situation - the disease - can grow worse.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Our analysis will use the distinction between the ?exposed? and the) Tj T*
(?sheltered? sectors of the economy - a distinction that originates from) Tj T*
(Swedish analysis in the 1950s \(Meidner c.s.\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Dutch policy reaction - though with some lag - was a general) Tj T*
(restraint of wage growth. This reaction was motivated by reference to) Tj T*
(the so-called Vintaf model developed by Den Hartog and Tjan at the) Tj T*
(Central Planning Bureau - see Driehuis & Van der Zwan eds. \(1978\) and) Tj T*
(Driehuis, Fase & Den Hartog eds. \(1988\). [101] The direct assumption) Tj T*
(was that high wage costs cause the scrap of old vintages of the capital) Tj T*
(stock, resulting in an irreversible loss of capacity. The indirect) Tj T*
(presumption was that a relative reduction of production costs could) Tj T*
(compensate for the rise in the exchange rate, restoring competitiveness) Tj T*
(and employment. [102]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(However, in a quite brilliant exposition that up to now has been) Tj T*
(neglected to the shame of the Dutch economics profession, Marein van) Tj T*
(Schaaijk \(1983\) of the same Bureau showed that a general wage restraint) Tj T*
