(neglects the fact that the exposed and sheltered sectors have a) Tj T*
(different composition of their labour force, with important effects. He) Tj T*
(noted that the exposed sector is industrial and has the larger share of) Tj T*
(well educated, highly productive or high value added labour; while the) Tj T*
(sheltered sector concerns services and has the larger share of lowly) Tj T*
(educated, lowly productive or low value added labour. A uniform wage) Tj T*
(restraint - targetted at reducing unemployment rather than balance on) Tj T*
(the external account - is too high for the exposed sector and thus) Tj T*
(subsidises exports; and the restraint is too low for the sheltered) Tj T*
(sector and thus generates unemployment. The restraint of incomes also) Tj T*
(means a restraint of imports, aggravating the situation. So Van) Tj T*
(Schaaijk noted in fact both the internal and the external imbalance,) Tj T*
(recognised that these mirrored each other, and that these were) Tj T*
(prolongued, now not by the original energy price hike but instead by) Tj T*
(policy.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Indeed, Holland since then has a strong external position - exporting) Tj T*
(unemployment to Europe - and a high internal unemployment - where the) Tj T*
(unemployment is hidden in ?disability? \(and hence registered by dull) Tj T*
(statisticians as ?low participation?\). Some surplus of the external) Tj T*
(account is reasonable given the natural resource, and the capital flows) Tj T*
(for foreign investments are useful for when the resource is depleted.) Tj T*
(But the Dutch external surplus is excessive.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Van Schaaijk?s suggested remedy was standard and sound. It was and is) Tj T*
(to let wages develop in line with productivity. Since Dutch policy is) Tj T*
(oriented to maintaining a more equal distribution of income - which) Tj T*
(explains part of the policy drive to see a _uniform_ development) Tj T*
(in wages - Van Schaaijk advised to use tax policy to correct the) Tj T*
(differential development of gross wages for its effect on net incomes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(However, as said, Van Schaaijk?s analysis has been neglected to this) Tj T*
(day, and Holland now suffers from a long period of unemployment_ and_ a) Tj T*
(trade surplus _and _a general restraint of wages and net incomes. There) Tj T*
(is a curious ?consistency? in the delusion with policy makers, that) Tj T*
(incomes restraint is required to maintain employment by generating a) Tj T*
(trade surplus, since, by restraining the home market, most Dutch) Tj T*
(employment growth seems dependent upon trade indeed. Strangely,) Tj T*
(economic developments caused the Central Planning Bureau to drop the) Tj T*
(Den Hartog & Tjan model in the mid 1980s, but the policy of wage) Tj T*
(restraint remained.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the 1982-1991 period I worked at the Central Planning Bureau too,) Tj T*
(and had the opportunity to get acquinted - albeit around 1986 only -) Tj T*
(with Van Schaaijk?s analysis. Apart from being enlightening by it) Tj T*
(itself, it opened my eyes - even while it was standard - to the) Tj T*
(importance of tax policy for unemployment, and thereby led to my papers) Tj T*
(\(Colignatus \(1989-1996\)\) and this present book, on the solution to the) Tj T*
(current mass unemployment in the OECD countries in general.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In my papers I have always referred to Van Schaaijk?s 1983 article) Tj T*
(whenever it was proper. However, in this chapter I have occasion to) Tj T*
