() Tj T*
(Governments, especially European ones, have been experimenting since) Tj T*
(World War II with all kinds of methods to control investments, but have) Tj T*
(been confronted with two major outcomes: \(a\) unemployment remained) Tj T*
(high, \(b\) many investments were considered failures. The economic) Tj T*
(paradigm since the Reagan years has been to let investments be) Tj T*
(determined by the market. Also Dutch social democrats like Wim Kok) Tj T*
(supported this approach, since it was thought that employment depended) Tj T*
(upon growth while growth depended upon the best investments that the) Tj T*
(market could provide. This paradigm led to reduced government outlays,) Tj T*
(less fiddling in the market, privatisation, and reduced taxes for the) Tj T*
(wealthy who were assumed to do the investing. The 1990s showed the boom) Tj T*
(associated with silicon valley - though should properly be associated) Tj T*
(also with this policy and the implementation of new financial) Tj T*
(instruments. But the boom went bust and the world was reminded of the) Tj T*
(logic of Keynes?s depression economics, see Krugman \(1999\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The point of criticism is that employment and growth are rather) Tj T*
(separate issues. Our own analysis in this book shows that a return to) Tj T*
(full employment is possible. The main instrument is to get rid of the) Tj T*
(tax void. Employment does not depend upon growth per se but employment) Tj T*
(depends upon a properly working system to allocate the work that is) Tj T*
(being done in an economy. Growth comes only into the story when we) Tj T*
(aspire at higher welfare by means of higher productivity. If we don?t) Tj T*
(want growth, we can easily imagine a stagnant economy. That said, most) Tj T*
(economies aspire at a growth in welfare. We can do this by designing) Tj T*
(new products or by material investments or by creative ways to) Tj T*
(reorganise production. [104] Then the problem returns of optimising) Tj T*
(investments that define real savings. Since some sections of the) Tj T*
(economy are devoted to investments, there is also the Keynesian) Tj T*
(phenomenon that investments influence activity, income and nominal) Tj T*
(savings.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The paradigm to ?minimize? the role of government in investment was) Tj T*
(misguided since the relation between growth and employment was) Tj T*
(misspecified. Now that we know that the tax void was the main cause of) Tj T*
(stagflation we can reconsider the paradigm. The argument that remains) Tj T*
(is that government meddling supposedly caused failed investments. The) Tj T*
(answer to that argument is \(i\) that failures must be judged on a) Tj T*
(case-by-case manner, by Cost Benefit Analysis, and \(ii\) that one should) Tj T*
(include the concept of Keynesian recession and that some investments) Tj T*
(might seem a failure but actually are beneficial. Note that there is no) Tj T*
(need for a government deficit since the analysis on the dynamic) Tj T*
(marginal rate shows that progressive taxes need not be a drawback for) Tj T*
(the richer. If growth is the issue, then the true issue is its) Tj T*
(optimality in terms of level and composition and effects.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The line of thought that I would suggest is that this optimum requires) Tj T*
(competing investment banks that develop plans during the economic) Tj T*
(upswing that can be implemented during the economic downswing. Who) Tj T*
(worries about pensions and the EU Lissabon Strategy is advised to) Tj T*
(consider this approach. Since the market is an anonymous beast that may) Tj T*
