(argument.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Over the years an ?accepted view? has grown in economics concerning the) Tj T*
(meaning of Arrow?s Theorem. This accepted view however has also implied) Tj T*
(a kind of moral stagnation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There are two main reasons to reconsider the accepted wisdom on the) Tj T*
(meaning of the Theorem and to rekindle the debate on it. The first) Tj T*
(reason is destructive, since it rejects Arrow?s position; the second) Tj T*
(reason is constructive, since it provides an alternative.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(These reasons are: \(1\) There is a distinction between the mathematical) Tj T*
(framework on one hand and its interpretation on the other hand. The) Tj T*
(Theorem holds, and the impossibility holds for Arrow?s axioms, but the) Tj T*
(questions of reasonableness and moral desirability are of a different) Tj T*
(kind. \(2\) The area of application of Arrow?s axioms seems rather) Tj T*
(static, while reality is dynamic. By considering the role of time,) Tj T*
(there is more scope for morality, and then one can identify a voting) Tj T*
(procedure that many would find attractive.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The two following chapters develop these arguments subsequently.) Tj T*
(Readers interested in more details are referred to Colignatus \(2001\),) Tj T*
(?Voting Theory for Democracy?. That book develops the theory of direct) Tj T*
(single seat elections from the bottom up while it also provides) Tj T*
(programs \(in _Mathematica_\) to eliminate the tedious work of the) Tj T*
(calculations of the various voting procedures.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(34. The solution to Arrow?s difficulty in social choice) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Summary) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Arrow?s Theorem holds that no constitution can satisfy certain) Tj T*
(properties. In annex to that theorem, Arrow claims that those) Tj T*
(properties are reasonable and morally desirable. In Arrow?s view there) Tj T*
(thus is the difficulty that people desire a constitution that cannot) Tj T*
(exist. While the Theorem stands as a mathematical result, the) Tj T*
(additional claims concern some other matters, namely the domains of) Tj T*
(reasonableness and morality. It are these claims that have caused much) Tj T*
(confusion in the literature. It is shown here that the claims are) Tj T*
(unwarranted, since inconsistent properties are neither reasonable nor) Tj T*
(morally desirable. It is shown too that Arrow?s axiom of Pairwise) Tj T*
(Decision Making \(formerly known as the Independence of Irrelevant) Tj T*
(Alternatives\) is not realistic, and thus unattractive. We show the) Tj T*
(existence of some constitutions without that axiom that are consistent) Tj T*
(and might be optimal to many. The major error made by Arrow and his) Tj T*
(students is to mix up the context of scientific discovery and learning) Tj T*
(with the context of application to the real world by educated people.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Introduction) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Arrow \(1950, 1951, 1963\) showed that if certain properties are) Tj T*
(postulated for a constitution, then such a constitution would not) Tj T*
ET
endstream
