(exist. This result has been checked by numerous scholars, is accepted) Tj T*
(by this author, and thus stands as a mathematical theorem. In fact, we) Tj T*
(will give a short proof below.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Arrow also claimed, annex to the theorem, and_ this_ will be at issue) Tj T*
(here, that those properties would be reasonable and morally desirable.) Tj T*
(He recently repeated that claim in the Palgrave \(1988:125\). He writes:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?\(...\) conditions to be imposed on constitutions \(...\)?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?\(...\) there is no social choice mechanism which satisfies a number of) Tj T*
(reasonable conditions?.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(For clarity it is useful to introduce the following abbreviations for) Tj T*
(the theorem and its companion claims, and their conjunction:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(            _AT _ = the Arrow Theorem) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(            _ARC_ = the Arrow Reasonableness Claim = the properties are) Tj T*
(            _reasonable_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(            _AMC_ = the Arrow Moral Claim = that they are _to be) Tj T*
(            imposed_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_            AGV _= the Arrow General View = _AT & ARC & AMC_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Note that Arrow?s phrasing on _ARC_ and _AMC_ is a bit ambiguous. The) Tj T*
(?to be imposed? might not be moral but merely logical, in a sense that) Tj T*
(one needs at least some conditions to make a constitution. However, the) Tj T*
(topic of collective choice is distinctly a moral one. Secondly, Arrow) Tj T*
(emphasises what is to be imposed and what is reasonable, but he may not) Tj T*
(be in a position to impose his views and morals on us. The best) Tj T*
