(Control of natural forces in the social process) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(One important idea is that time plays a role. The basis for this idea) Tj T*
(is that, abstractly, morality presupposes time. Without time there) Tj T*
(would be no morality. In a static world everything is given, and there) Tj T*
(is no place for an individual who has to ponder his or her moral) Tj T*
(choices. As economists, we can draw static utility functions and) Tj T*
(isoquants, but those are abstractions, and they might distract from the) Tj T*
(real moral problem. The moral problem is that _now_ a decision has to) Tj T*
(be made while the consequences appear _later_. Afterwards, everything) Tj T*
(can be explained deterministically \(which is the meaning of) Tj T*
(?explanation?\), and by hypothesis, determinism will also hold for the) Tj T*
(future. Yet, in the mean time forecasts are imperfect, there is) Tj T*
(fundamental uncertainty, and that creates the possibility of morality) Tj T*
(\(or the illusion of morality\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Economic science is intended to help explain reality. In this reality,) Tj T*
(we see an evolution of human beings in a social process of natural) Tj T*
(forces. The basic concept is power, in a continuous process, so that) Tj T*
(the basic approach uses ratio scales and cardinal utility and not) Tj T*
(ordinal scales. Other assumptions than cardinality enter the discussion) Tj T*
(_only_ when the group wants to control power, and for example introduce) Tj T*
(democracy. A common notion is that economists reject cardinality and) Tj T*
(interpersonal comparison of utility. However, the concept of ?one) Tj T*
(person, one vote? actually imposes some interpersonal comparison of) Tj T*
(utilities. Also comparing orderings of preferences implies some) Tj T*
(comparison of utilities. The proper perspective is rather that) Tj T*
(cardinality is deficient since people can cheat about their preferences) Tj T*
(\(at least in the current state of technology\). The major argument for) Tj T*
(ordinality is that it limits the room for cheating. If people could not) Tj T*
(cheat, interpersonal comparison likely would be much more popular) Tj T*
(amongst economists. The point that ordinality reduces interpersonal) Tj T*
(comparison thus seems less relevant than the point that cardinal) Tj T*
(comparisons are unreliable since people can cheat.) Tj T*
