(Three traditional methods) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In Plurality, all voters have one vote, and the candidate with the) Tj T*
(highest number is selected. Note the problems with this method. The) Tj T*
(criterion of ?highest number? does not imply that the winner must also) Tj T*
(have more than 50% of the vote. If this is additionally imposed, then) Tj T*
(this may require more rounds of voting, and then there is the difficult) Tj T*
(issue whether candidates have to drop out, and if so, how.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Borda?s method is to let each voter rank the candidates by importance,) Tj T*
(then assign weights given by the rank position, to add the weights per) Tj T*
(candidate for all voters, and then select the candidate with the) Tj T*
(highest value. Note that the method appears sensitive to preference) Tj T*
(reversal, see below.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Condorcet?s method is to vote on all pairs of candidates, and to select) Tj T*
(the one who wins from all alternatives. Note that such a ?Condorcet) Tj T*
(winner? does not need to exist. In that case the margins of winning can) Tj T*
(be used to solve the deadlock - but this increases the sensitivity to) Tj T*
(who participates.) Tj T*
