(Another consequence of the switch of attention from statics to dynamics) Tj T*
(is the recognition of a _status quo_.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There appears to exist another wide-spread confusion about ?majority) Tj T*
(voting?. This idea is that a majority result would still be) Tj T*
(democratically valid, even if the winning decision implies a real loss) Tj T*
(for the opposition. The counter-example is when the majority decides) Tj T*
(that the minority pays $1 to the majority: this is not necessarily a) Tj T*
(morally acceptable situation, even though there is a majority. From a) Tj T*
(moral point of view, each voting scheme should have two rounds: a first) Tj T*
(round to select the Pareto improving points compared to the _status) Tj T*
(quo_, and then a second round to select the winner from those Paretian) Tj T*
(improvements. The majority rule thus can be regarded as only a) Tj T*
(tie-breaking rule, namely for the deadlock when there are more Pareto) Tj T*
(improving points. In elections of persons, the status quo can be a) Tj T*
(vacancy, and in that respect all candidates could be taken as Paretian.) Tj T*
