(One argument against the selection of Pareto improving points is that) Tj T*
(people might also cheat about these points. This argument is not) Tj T*
(convincing, since Pareto improvement is in one?s own interest. Indeed,) Tj T*
(little Robby might try to veto Spain by saying that he does not want a) Tj T*
(holiday, and thus he might be trying to bargain to get everybody to) Tj T*
(accept Greece. However, this ploy can be prevented by having that first) Tj T*
(round on having a holiday, since if he really wants a holiday anyhow,) Tj T*
(then he has to show this then. Careful construction of the voting) Tj T*
(process thus remains an issue.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A note on cheating) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(One of the key problems in voting theory is strategic voting behaviour,) Tj T*
(better known as cheating. In a scheme like Borda, cardinal utility has) Tj T*
(already been reduced to ordinal utility, so perhaps we should be) Tj T*
(lenient and allow voters to maximize their utility from the final) Tj T*
(outcome by manipulating their vote. But our opinion on this does not) Tj T*
(matter, since the ballot generally is secret and we cannot stop people) Tj T*
(from voting strategically anyway. In fact, my _Mathematica _programs,) Tj T*
(Colignatus \(2001\), contain routines for cheating. These are simple) Tj T*
(routines that assume both full information and that others don?t cheat,) Tj T*
(since the mathematics of cheating while assuming that others cheat too) Tj T*
(is rather complex, especially when nobody has full information about) Tj T*
(the true preferences. Given all this, one surmises that election) Tj T*
(results do not reflect the true state.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Thinking about these issues gave me an idea that might be helpful to) Tj T*
(elicit the true state. Suppose that each voter is informed in advance) Tj T*
(that there is a probability _p _that the ranking order that is) Tj T*
(submitted will be used by the election computer for strategic voting.) Tj T*
(If the voter submits his or her true ranking, then this is rewarded) Tj T*
(with probability _p_ to improve the election result for that voter, and) Tj T*
(much better than the voter can, since the computer knows all submitted) Tj T*
(rankings. If the voter submits a strategically adapted ranking, then) Tj T*
(this is punished with probability _p _namely to improve the election) Tj T*
(result for that false ranking. Likely there is a specific value of _p) Tj T*
