(issue. Of course, if you are a victim of such ?doom books? then you) Tj T*
(might benefit from Cox & Alm?s exposition, but then you shouldn?t) Tj T*
(forget about the serious literature, and the authors should warn about) Tj T*
(that.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(One of the reasons why the book is unbalanced is that it seems to serve) Tj T*
(two goals. On one hand the argument seems to be that America is doing) Tj T*
(well ?on average? \(and even for the majority of the people\) and on the) Tj T*
(other hand the argument seems to be that the poor are not as poor as) Tj T*
(claimed. This creates the contortion that, when it is shown that the) Tj T*
(average American home now contains many electronic gadgets, there) Tj T*
(apparently is also the suggestion that this would be true for the poor) Tj T*
(- while this certainly cannot be the case. Conversely, where it is) Tj T*
(argued that many of the legally poor actually are retired people with) Tj T*
($300,000 valued homes, then this indeed is useful to note \(and points) Tj T*
(to a possible error in America?s laws\) but it doesn?t clarify anything) Tj T*
(about the working poor.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
