(In any event, stress on differences should not obscure the fact that) Tj T*
(all sciences, including even economies, agree on many things. There is,) Tj T*
(besides, an enormous store of historical and descriptive) Tj T*
(matter?economic facts?that is well worth knowing and concerning which) Tj T*
(there is little dispute. We shall hope that the burden placed on the) Tj T*
(reader of suspending judgment and viewing the same things in different) Tj T*
(lights will not be too heavy.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(One of the dominant schools of the day looks upon economics as study of) Tj T*
(what happens when we try to reconcile the scarcity of things with the) Tj T*
(insatiable wants of human beings. Most things worth having, except the) Tj T*
(air we breathe are scarce ? scarce enough, at least, to command a price) Tj T*
(and not to be available to all in generous quantity. Among the less) Tj T*
(dominant and dissenting schools is one that considers the study of the) Tj T*
(disposal of scarce goods too restrictive. Some mem­bers of this class) Tj T*
(focus their interest on the moral codes, business practices, social) Tj T*
(instimtions, legal framework, and the like under which we get our food,) Tj T*
(clothing, and shelter. They study an economic system ? capitalism, for) Tj T*
(example ? in much the same way that an anthropologist studies the) Tj T*
(Klamath Indians or some primitive tribe of a South Sea island. They) Tj T*
(ask, and try to answer, questions that have little to do with the) Tj T*
(disposal of scarce goods.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The student may find it helpful to examine the definitions given below.) Tj T*
(They represent the thought of several periods and schools. In these) Tj T*
(definitions the older phrase ?political economy? is more or less equal) Tj T*
(to the modern word ?economics.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Oeconomy, in general, is the art of providing for all the wants of a) Tj T*
(family, with prudence and frugality ?. What oeconomy is in a family,) Tj T*
(political oeconomy is in a state \(Sir James Steuart, 1712-1780\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Writers on Political Economy profess to teach, or to investigate, the) Tj T*
(nature of Wealth, and the laws of production and distribution:) Tj T*
(including directly or remotely, the operation of all causes by which) Tj T*
(the condition of mankind, or of any society of human beings, in respect) Tj T*
(to this universal object of human desire, is made prosperous or the) Tj T*
(reverse \(John Stuart Mill, 1896-73\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Political Economy treats chiefly of the material interests of nations.) Tj T*
(It inquires how the various wants of the people of a country,) Tj T*
(especially those of food, clothing, fuel, shelter, of the sexual) Tj T*
(instinct etc., may be satisfied; how the satisfaction of these wants) Tj T*
(influences the aggregate national life, and how in turn, they are) Tj T*
(influenced by the national life \(Wilhelm Roscher, 1817-94\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Political Economy or Economics is a study of mankind in the ordinary) Tj T*
(business of life; it examines that part of individual and social action) Tj T*
(which is most closely connected with the attainment and with the use of) Tj T*
(the material requisites of well being. Thus it is on the one side a) Tj T*
(study of wealth; and on the other, and more important side, a part of) Tj T*
(the study of man \(Alfred Marshall, 1842?1924\).) Tj T*
ET
