stream
BT /F1 10 Tf 54 738 Td 13 TL
(\(1748; trans. 1750\), in which he examined the three main types of) Tj T*
(government \(republic, monarchy, and despotism\) and states that a) Tj T*
(relationship does exist between an area?s climate, geography, and) Tj T*
(general circumstances and the form of government that evolves.) Tj T*
(Montesquieu also held that governmental powers should be separated and) Tj T*
(balanced to guarantee individual rights and freedom.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Note that his original name was Secondat, and that he inherited the) Tj T*
(title of Baron from his uncle in 1716. He also was elected to the Royal) Tj T*
(Society in 1730. See http://tqd.advanced.org/3376/Monty2.htm) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Sir Isaiah Berlin: ?Montesquieu advocated constitutionalism, the) Tj T*
(preservation of civil liberties, the abolition of slavery, gradualism,) Tj T*
(moderation, peace, internationalism, social and economic justice with) Tj T*
(due respect to national and local tradition. He believed in justice and) Tj T*
(the rule of law; detested all forms of extremism and fanaticism; put) Tj T*
(his faith in the balance of power and the division of authority as a) Tj T*
(weapon against despotic rule by individuals or groups or majorities;) Tj T*
(and approved of social equality, but not the point which it threatened) Tj T*
(individual liberty; and out of liberty, but not to the point where it) Tj T*
(threatened to disrupt orderly government.? \(?Against the Current?\)) Tj T*
(\(Also taken from the internet.\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Spirit of Laws can actually be read on the internet at) Tj T*
(http://www.constitution.org/) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I?ve read the introductory parts, and find them still quite readable.) Tj T*
(One notes that Montesquieu refers to the ?laws of the material world?,) Tj T*
(and one cannot but think that Newton \(1642-1727\) has some influence) Tj T*
(here.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is interesting too what Montesquieu has to say on economics \(Book) Tj T*
(XX.7\):) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?Other nations have made the interests of commerce yield to those of) Tj T*
(politics; the English, on the contrary, have ever made their political) Tj T*
(interests give way to those of commerce. They know better than any) Tj T*
(other people upon earth how to value, at the same time, these three) Tj T*
(great advantages -- religion, commerce, and liberty.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Also interesting is what he writes on taxes:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?12. Relation between the Weight of Taxes and Liberty. It is a general) Tj T*
(rule that taxes may be heavier in proportion to the liberty of the) Tj T*
(subject, and that there is a necessity for reducing them in proportion) Tj T*
(to the increase of slavery. This has always been and always will be the) Tj T*
(case. It is a rule derived from nature that never varies. We find it in) Tj T*
(all parts -- in England, in Holland, and in every state where liberty) Tj T*
(gradually declines, till we come to Turkey.? \(Book XIII.12\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Also:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
ET
