stream
BT /F1 10 Tf 54 738 Td 13 TL
(?Thus, in the Roman world, as at Sparta, the freemen enjoyed the) Tj T*
(highest degree of liberty, while those who were slaves laboured under) Tj T*
(the extremity of servitude.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(While the citizens paid taxes, they were raised with great justice and) Tj T*
(equality. The regulation of Servius Tullius was observed, who had) Tj T*
(distributed the people into six classes, according to their difference) Tj T*
(of property, and fixed the several shares of the public imposts in) Tj T*
(proportion to that which each person had in the government. Hence they) Tj T*
(bore with the greatness of the tax because of their proportionable) Tj T*
(greatness of credit, and consoled themselves for the smallness of their) Tj T*
(credit because of the smallness of the tax.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There was also another thing worthy of admiration, which is, that as) Tj T*
(Servius Tullius?s division into classes was in some measure the) Tj T*
(fundamental principle of the constitution, it thence followed that an) Tj T*
(equal levying of the taxes was so connected with this fundamental) Tj T*
(principle that the one could not be abolished without the other.?) Tj T*
(\(Book XI.19\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(He discusses exemption of taxes for whole provinces.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(18. Of an Exemption from Taxes. The maxim of the great eastern empires,) Tj T*
(of exempting such provinces as have very much suffered from taxes,) Tj T*
(ought to be extended to monarchical states. There are some, indeed,) Tj T*
(where this practice is established; yet the country is more oppressed) Tj T*
(than if no such rule took place; because as the prince levies still) Tj T*
(neither more nor less, the state becomes bound for the whole. In order) Tj T*
(to ease a village that pays badly, they load another that pays better;) Tj T*
(the former is not relieved, and the latter is ruined. The people grow) Tj T*
(desperate, between the necessity of paying for fear of exactions, and) Tj T*
(the danger of paying for fear of new burdens. \(XIII.18\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(On exemption we also find something like a ?basic income? for nobles:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?We find in Xenophon?s Banquet a very lively description of a republic) Tj T*
(in which the people abused their equality. Each guest gives in his turn) Tj T*
(the reason why he is satisfied. ?Content I am,? says Chamides, ?because) Tj T*
(of my poverty. When I was rich, I was obliged to pay my court to) Tj T*
(informers, knowing I was more liable to be hurt by them than capable of) Tj T*
(doing them harm. The republic constantly demanded some new tax of me;) Tj T*
(and I could not decline paying. Since I have grown poor, I have) Tj T*
(acquired authority; nobody threatens me; I rather threaten others. I) Tj T*
(can go or stay where I please. The rich already rise from their seats) Tj T*
(and give me the way. I am a king, I was before a slave: I paid taxes to) Tj T*
(the republic, now it maintains me: I am no longer afraid of losing: but) Tj T*
(I hope to acquire.?? \(Book VIII.2\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
