(forward by others. On some occasions, when it was felt that such) Tj T*
(written summaries were inadequate, the group would meet with the) Tj T*
(president to present opposing views.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This process gave the CEA an opportunity to influence both the specific) Tj T*
(decisions and the way that members of the administration thought about) Tj T*
(particular issues. This was true at every level from the departmental) Tj T*
(senior staff that interacted with the CEA economists to the cabinet) Tj T*
(level.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In addition to these relatively large group meetings with the) Tj T*
(President, there were also smaller meetings dealing with specific) Tj T*
(subjects. A central organizing set of meetings each year dealt with the) Tj T*
(budget. Here the only regular participants, in addition to the) Tj T*
(president and the vice-president, were the Secretary of the Treasury,) Tj T*
(the Director of the Office of Management and Budget \(OMB\), the Chairman) Tj T*
(of the CEA, and a small number of senior White House staff. The series) Tj T*
(of budget meetings began with a five-year economic forecast prepared by) Tj T*
(the CEA. Technical staff discussions and meetings between a CEA member,) Tj T*
(a Treasury assistant secretary and an associate director of the OMB) Tj T*
(would review the evidence on which a forecast would be based. In) Tj T*
(insisted, however, that the CEA alone was responsible for the final) Tj T*
(forecast in order to avoid a repetition of earlier experience in which) Tj T*
(the forecast was widely \(and correctly\) criticized as over-optimistic,) Tj T*
(and therefore as leading to a substantial underestimate to future) Tj T*
(budget deficits. Needless to say, this was a source of friction and) Tj T*
(contention.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Other such small meetings with the president included preparation for) Tj T*
(the G-7 economic summits, for his televised national press conferences,) Tj T*
(and for discussions of special subjects like social security reform.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Secretary of the Treasury and I also met roughly every two weeks) Tj T*
(with the president and a few senior White House staff to discuss) Tj T*
(subjects of our choice. The Treasury Secretary frequently used these) Tj T*
(sessions to discuss monetary policy or issues currently under) Tj T*
(development at the Treasury. I frequently discussed the budget deficit) Tj T*
(but also talked about things like the character of unemployment, the) Tj T*
(nature of the trade imbalance, and other types of general ?background?) Tj T*
(information. These were not intended as decision-making sessions.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In addition to these meetings, I also sent the president brief memos on) Tj T*
(particular issues. Occasionally these would be my thoughts on some) Tj T*
(issue being discussed in the administration. There were also almost) Tj T*
(daily brief memos telling the President how to interpret important) Tj T*
(economic statistics that would be released the next morning so that he) Tj T*
(would not be caught unaware of the information \(by the press or other) Tj T*
(visitors\) or uninformed about the significance \(or lack of) Tj T*
(significance\) of the particular statistic.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The CEA also serves as a source of professional economic advice to) Tj T*
(other departments and agencies. In some cases, this serves to reinforce) Tj T*
ET
endstream
