(\(1\) Colignatus \(1996d\) ?An institutional explanation of structural) Tj T*
(unemployment of low income labour?, presentation for the Dutch ?7th) Tj T*
(Research Day of the Social Sciences?, Amsterdam, ewp-oth/9605001. The) Tj T*
(idea of this paper is to use results of social psychology to identify) Tj T*
(the real forces implied by the reduced form theorems. The paper?s) Tj T*
(summary is:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?Structural unemployment of low income labour has causes in) Tj T*
(institutional settings. Directly, there is a systematic error in the) Tj T*
(co-ordination of employment policy and tax policy. Indirectly, the) Tj T*
(system of co-ordination shows a deficiency in its capacity to repair) Tj T*
(systematic errors.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Many people see the cause of mass unemployment in technology and) Tj T*
(?globalisation?, which are factors on the demand side. Others see the) Tj T*
(cause in high benefit levels or in low levels of education or) Tj T*
(educationability, which are factors on the supply side. These) Tj T*
(explanations allow little room for policy making, especially when the) Tj T*
(benefit level is regarded as social subsistence. There however is a) Tj T*
(third explanation, one that has been put forward by employees of the) Tj T*
(Dutch Central Planning Bureau \(CPB\), first Van Schaaijk in 1983, then) Tj T*
(Bakhoven in 1988 and Colignatus in 1989-1996. In this approach the) Tj T*
(cause of unemployment must be found in policies on taxes and social) Tj T*
(security, an area where policy can do a lot. In this third approach,) Tj T*
(technology and trade have reduced the problem of unemployment, since) Tj T*
(they have boosted productivity. Since the problem lies with labour) Tj T*
(costs and the demand for labour, supply factors like the benefit level) Tj T*
(are less relevant. This third approach does not attract much attention.) Tj T*
(The three authors are little known, even though they at the time worked) Tj T*
(at a renowned institute.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This paper intends to raise the attention level towards asking the) Tj T*
(proper questions about current stagnation. The best way to tackle) Tj T*
(stagnation likely is the institutional approach. The economy and its) Tj T*
(management can be regarded as a _system, _which system comprises the) Tj T*
(community of economists, officials, politicians, journalists and ?the) Tj T*
(general public?. This paper then proceeds by using Aronson?s book on) Tj T*
(social psychology to discuss various properties of the system and) Tj T*
(relations within it, and the behaviour of the participants in the) Tj T*
(collective decision making on this complex issue. The discussion) Tj T*
(results into a number of questions for further research.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(\(2\) Colignatus \(1998c\), ?On the paradox of efficiency improvement at) Tj T*
(the micro level and Productivity Slowdown at the macro level: The case) Tj T*
(of Efficient Inventory Control?, ewp-get/9805003. The summary is:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?Last decades show a Productivity Slowdown at the macro level, while at) Tj T*
(the micro level we have seen a huge attention for business economics) Tj T*
(and operations management - and we now have a decade of booming stock) Tj T*
(markets. This paper tries to tackle that paradox by singling out the) Tj T*
(issue of Efficient Inventory Control. This seems to be the part of the) Tj T*
