(Autobiographical note) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This book completes a project that started in 1989 and that is closely) Tj T*
(related to the Fall of the Berlin Wall in that year.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(At that time in 1989, and in fact from 1982-1991, I was employed as a) Tj T*
(?economic scientific researcher? at the Dutch Central Planning Bureau) Tj T*
(\(CPB\), which institute can be compared to the US Council of Economic) Tj T*
(Advisers. The CPB provides the executive branch with economic) Tj T*
(projections and with evaluations of policy proposals. In 1989 I was) Tj T*
(involved in test runs for a study of the economy for the long run till) Tj T*
(2015, later published as the CPB \(1992a&b\) ?Netherlands in Triplo? and) Tj T*
(?Scanning the future?. The test runs showed continued economic) Tj T*
(problems, and this caused me to consider some points. If the Bureau) Tj T*
(would publish bad weather projections, then these might cause the) Tj T*
(government to enact economic reforms that would self-unfulfill the) Tj T*
(projections. Secondly, my CPB colleagues Van Schaaijk \(1983\) and) Tj T*
(Bakhoven \(1988\) had presented a solution approach to unemployment that) Tj T*
(did not get the attention that it deserved. Thirdly, when the Wall) Tj T*
(fell, it was obvious that continued unemployment in Western Europe) Tj T*
(would be detrimental to economic recovery in the East, and this) Tj T*
