(itself and the citizen?s right to be properly informed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Keynes?s _General Theory_ can be generalised even further by the) Tj T*
(inclusion of endogenous government in the model, and in particular) Tj T*
(economic policy making itself as that is guided by economic theory.) Tj T*
(Keynes clearly anticipated this line of thinking, where he wrote:) Tj T*
(?Practical men, who believe themselves to be quite exempt from any) Tj T*
(intellectual influences, are usually the slaves of some defunct) Tj T*
(economist. Madmen in authority, who hear voices in the air, are) Tj T*
(distilling their frenzy from some academic scribbler of a few years) Tj T*
(back.? \(GT:383\) The new point now is that this does not only concern) Tj T*
(?practical men? but economists themselves too, and the whole) Tj T*
(institutional framework for economic advice. When economic policy) Tj T*
(making itself is part of the model, economic stagnation can be) Tj T*
(explained as stagnation in that realm, and the solution for economic) Tj T*
(stagnation can be found there too.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(OECD nations had full employment in the 1950-1970 period, and Japan and) Tj T*
(Sweden had it much longer. So it would seem that full employment at) Tj T*
(least is feasible. However, after the period of full employment, all) Tj T*
(nations showed the phenomenon of stagflation, which is a worsening) Tj T*
(trade-off between inflation and unemployment \(represented as the shift) Tj T*
(of the Phillipscurve\), frequently associated with stagnating growth.) Tj T*
(Instead of full employment and a steady growth of welfare, OECD nations) Tj T*
(suffered a long period of insecurity from 1970-2005.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This volume analyses the different periods and finds the likely cause.) Tj T*
(The fundamental cause is the common Trias Politica structure of) Tj T*
(economic decision making that all OECD nations share over time and) Tj T*
(space. At an operational level, stagflation can be explained by the tax) Tj T*
(policy that OECD nations have in common as well.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The common tax policy is based upon a particular economic theory that) Tj T*
(has become the conventional economic view of our time. This) Tj T*
(conventional theory sees tax as a penalty on work effort and holds that) Tj T*
(statutory marginal rates have major disincentive effects. Marginal tax) Tj T*
(rates are a useful penalty on \(inflationary\) wage claims in) Tj T*
(wage-bargaining, but the conventional view is that the disincentive) Tj T*
(effect dominates. Following this theory, policy has been to reduce) Tj T*
(marginal rates at the cost of lower exemption. Another measure was to) Tj T*
(switch from the income tax to a Value Added Tax \(VAT\) that has no) Tj T*
(exemption at all.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The common tax policy has static and dynamic components. Statically,) Tj T*
(exemption is low. Dynamically, there is the tendency of reducing) Tj T*
(exemption even further. The low and ever lower exemption causes rising) Tj T*
(tax levels and hence either poverty or higher labour costs in the lower) Tj T*
(wage brackets, causing unemployment, and causing higher taxes to pay) Tj T*
(for the benefits. What is crucially wrong about current policies is the) Tj T*
(phenomenon of differential indexation. Exemption is indexed on) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
685 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 684 0 R >>
endobj
686 0 obj
<< /Length 3674 >>
stream
BT /F1 10 Tf 54 738 Td 13 TL
(inflation, while subsistence, by social psychological causes, rises) Tj T*
(with inflation and real income. This differential indexation causes) Tj T*
(ever increasing problems with poverty and unemployment.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The OECD countries have been pursueing this policy now for more than) Tj T*
(three decades, and rather little is being achieved. It is time to) Tj T*
(seriously wonder whether policy is on the right track. This book shows) Tj T*
(where the conventional theory goes wrong.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A first feature is the tax void. The tax void is the region of) Tj T*
(productivity and income between the net minimum wage and the gross) Tj T*
(minimum wage. The difference between net and gross is normally called a) Tj T*
(?tax wedge?, but this term is inadequate since a wedge is commonly) Tj T*
(thought to apply _at_ a particular level while the void is a _range_.) Tj T*
(The income range between the net and gross minimum wage is a void since) Tj T*
(there are official tax statutes for that range but  no true revenues.) Tj T*
(People are not allowed to work below the gross minimum and thus cannot) Tj T*
(pay taxes there \(that is, for full timers\). Ideally, as in the 1950s,) Tj T*
(the net minimum should be equal to the gross minimum so that the void) Tj T*
(is zero, and so that such workers can start earning their own living) Tj T*
(without paying taxes. Because of the current practices for tax) Tj T*
(indexation, the tax void has grown over time so that the gross minimum) Tj T*
(wage has risen much more than the net minimum wage. By result, more and) Tj T*
(more low wage workers are subject to that excessively high gross) Tj T*
(minimum and are effectively removed from the labour market. The shift) Tj T*
(of the Phillipscurve can be explained partly by this growing component) Tj T*
(of minimum wage unemployment. This analysis also points to a solution.) Tj T*
(For the tax void, no taxes are collected \(on full timers\), thus) Tj T*
(abolishing such void taxes will not cost anything. The argument is not) Tj T*
(quite that lowering the minimum wage will create new job opportunities,) Tj T*
(but rather that not raising the gross wage costs so excesssively would) Tj T*
(not have destroyed the opportunities that already existed. This) Tj T*
(argument designs an experiment at no cost.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The tax void causes needless unemployment for millions of people all) Tj T*
(over the world and its plain bureaucratic stupidity is a blow to naive) Tj T*
(ideas about democracy \(that the current democratic structure would be) Tj T*
(adequate and provide adequate information\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The second feature in the new analysis concerns the dynamic marginal) Tj T*
(tax rate. Marginal tax rates are important - since economic theory) Tj T*
(indeed assumes optimising economic agents - but these marginal rates) Tj T*
(should be properly computed. This analysis not only considers the) Tj T*
(partial effect, assuming other things constant, but rather considers) Tj T*
(the total effect that includes all simultaneous changes. A change in a) Tj T*
(marginal tax rate is usually accompanied by a change in exemption, and) Tj T*
(both generally happen at the same time, either annually or in computer) Tj T*
(policy simulations. Private and national income change at the same time) Tj T*
(too. Individuals are frequently aware that their own fortunes are) Tj T*
(linked to the fortunes of the national economy and they will be) Tj T*
(sensitive to their relative position in the distribution of income.) Tj T*
(Work incentives may be more guided by the average tax rate rather than) Tj T*
ET
endstream
