688 0 obj
<< /Length 3305 >>
stream
BT /F1 10 Tf 54 738 Td 13 TL
(the statutory marginal tax rate. Hence, ?incentives? may not be a) Tj T*
(convincing argument against higher marginal tax rates, even though) Tj T*
(policy makers have been advancing that argument forcefully. That, in) Tj T*
(fact, the converse is true, fits perfectly with the experience of the) Tj T*
(last decades. The reduction of the statutory marginal rates, as the) Tj T*
(policy was, appears to have had little incentive effects, since the) Tj T*
(true incentive effect depends more on the average tax over time, and) Tj T*
(this average has remained high due to the problems of unemployment,) Tj T*
(poverty and lower growth.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This book concludes that macro-economic policies in OECD nations have) Tj T*
(not countered stagflation but have actually increased it. Current) Tj T*
(policies add to labour costs, reduce incentives, fuel forward shifting) Tj T*
(of the tax burden, and worsen the trade-off between inflation and) Tj T*
(unemployment.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The new analysis points directly to a policy that will be successful) Tj T*
(and that will allow a return to full employment under stable prices) Tj T*
(like in the the 1950s. If exemption is put at subsistence, then jobs) Tj T*
(can be created at the low end of the labour market, which would save) Tj T*
(benefits and reduce average taxes, which again would increase) Tj T*
(incentives. The alternative structure and policy would also be) Tj T*
(beneficial for inflation. If low productivity labour has a stronger) Tj T*
(position in the labour market, then the risk of unemployment is spread) Tj T*
(more evenly, and trend-setting high productivity labour will be) Tj T*
(cautious about wage claims.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A welfare state is defined as a state that doesn?t let people die and) Tj T*
(thus provides benefits for the lowly productive _anyway_. The welfare) Tj T*
(state can be run more efficiently by using those resources, instead of) Tj T*
(going into benefits, to instead reduce labour costs and to price the) Tj T*
(lowly productive into jobs. The analysis on inflation and unemployment) Tj T*
(thus results into the proposition that, since the present situation is) Tj T*
(inefficient, an improvement is possible from which everybody can) Tj T*
(benefit.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This book provides theorems in mathematical economics to prove its) Tj T*
(points. The central questions in the political economy of employment in) Tj T*
(the welfare state are: _can _one solve unemployment, does one _know) Tj T*
(_how, and does one _want _to ? The book presents a model that satisfies) Tj T*
(the stylized facts and thus serves theoretical and empirical uses.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(· The first result is a possibility theorem \(_can_\) that there are two) Tj T*
(regimes of either full employment or unemployment.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(· The second theorem explains the choice by _know_ and_ want _causes.) Tj T*
(Full employment results from conscious choice or chance \(while lacking) Tj T*
(knowledge\). Unemployment results from conscious choice or wrong) Tj T*
(co-ordination \(where a Pareto optimising change is blocked only by lack) Tj T*
(of knowledge - and a lack of knowledge not by the economists but by the) Tj T*
(incompetent or insensitive policy makers\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
ET
endstream
