([10] Vide Keynes?s very definition of ?effective demand?: what) Tj T*
(businesses expect to sell and thus are willing to currently produce,) Tj T*
(after taking account of already available stocks. ?Effective demand?) Tj T*
(thus is another word for ?production?. Key Keynesian is seeing) Tj T*
(production as an expectational variable.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([11] It must be recalled that more economists in the early 1900s turned) Tj T*
(from comparative statics to dynamics. A key figure is Tinbergen, who) Tj T*
(used the calculus of variations in his thesis and who presented the) Tj T*
(first macro-economic model that the world has seen \(see e.g. Boumans) Tj T*
(\(1992\) and Barten \(1988\)\). It may be noted that Tinbergen?s first) Tj T*
(national model does not contain a monetary sector. In a sense) Tj T*
(understandable, since the model was for Holland, and Holland was on the) Tj T*
(gold standard at that time, and we know - with Mundell, who refers to) Tj T*
(Tinbergen?s ?instrument argument? - that monetary policy in that case) Tj T*
(is ineffective. Anyway, Tinbergen clearly was more of a ?real business) Tj T*
(cycle? analyst, while Keynes had the feeling for monetary issues.) Tj T*
(Keynes?s approach appeared more important, primarily since money is a) Tj T*
(generic policy instrument for the whole \(world\) economy.) Tj T*
